  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you travel often and want unused data to roll over to your next trip, plus a real refund option if plans change. Pick <strong>UPeSIM</strong> if you just want a straightforward, cheaper prepaid plan for one trip and don't care about a safety net. They're both data-only prepaid eSIMs, but Yesim charges more for flexibility that UPeSIM doesn't offer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Rolling Balance vs. Fixed Block</h2>
  <p>
    Yesim's "Pay & Fly" plans work like a topped-up balance: you pay in dollars rather than a fixed GB amount, priced from $1.10/GB, and whatever's left over rolls into your next purchase instead of expiring. UPeSIM sells a plain fixed block of days or gigabytes that expires once it runs out, with no rollover and no auto-renewal, so there's never a lingering balance to manage either way.
  </p>
  <p>
    That difference matters most if you travel more than once a year. A Yesim balance you don't fully use on one trip is still there for the next one. A UPeSIM plan you don't finish is gone the moment it expires, full stop.
  </p>

