  <h2>Is Yesim or UPeSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You travel more than once a year and want leftover data to carry forward</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay more for a real refund option on an unused plan</li>
        <li>You don't mind paying by the day instead of by the gigabyte</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with UPeSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>It's a one-off trip and you don't need the plan to outlast it</li>
        <li>You want the cheaper per-GB rate and can live without a refund</li>
        <li>You'd rather set the eSIM up ahead of time and forget about it until you land</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yesim's refund policy is unusually generous.</strong> It advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans, not just ones that failed to activate. UPeSIM, like most prepaid eSIMs, treats a purchased plan as final once bought.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>UPeSIM's plan doesn't start counting down until you land.</strong> It only begins once your phone actually connects to the destination network, so you can install it well ahead of a trip. The catch: an installed-but-never-activated UPeSIM profile goes stale after 60 days, so don't set it up for a trip that's still months out. Yesim's balance model sidesteps that problem entirely since unused funds simply roll over rather than expiring on a fixed clock.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Yesim vs UPeSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Yesim or UPeSIM</h3>
      <p>UPeSIM, in most cases. Yesim sits in our "Expensive" bracket and UPeSIM in "Average," and UPeSIM's per-GB rate usually beats Yesim's per-day rate for a single trip.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for someone who travels several times a year</h3>
      <p>Yesim, mainly because of the rolling balance. Money left on a Yesim Pay &amp; Fly plan carries into your next purchase, where a UPeSIM plan expires and any leftover data is gone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund if I don't end up using the plan</h3>
      <p>Only with Yesim, which advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans. UPeSIM treats a purchase as final once it's bought.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do either of these include a phone number or calls</h3>
      <p>No. Both are data-only. Voice calls route over apps like WhatsApp on the data connection for both providers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

